(* (* You should get something like:

NAME       CPU(cores)   CPU(%)   MEMORY(bytes)   MEMORY(%)
minikube   200m         5%      1500Mi           20%

The exact numbers will be different on your machine. *)

For the SSH tunnel method, you do NOT need to open port 41949 in the EC2 Security Group.

You only need SSH access:

Inbound
  TCP
  22
  Your IP

You do not need:

41949

open publicly.

That's one of the major advantages of SSH tunneling.


Git includes its own ssh.exe. We just need to call that executable directly.

1. Check Git's SSH

Run this in your current PowerShell:

& "C:\Program Files\Git\usr\bin\ssh.exe" -V

You should get something like:

OpenSSH_9.xp1, OpenSSL ...
2. Create the SSH tunnel

Now run:

& "C:\Program Files\Git\usr\bin\ssh.exe" -i ".\k8s-north.pem" -L 37673:127.0.0.1:37673 ubuntu@3.81.136.250
3. First connection

If you see:

The authenticity of host '3.81.136.250' can't be established.
Are you sure you want to continue connecting (yes/no/[fingerprint])?

Type:

yes

Then you should get something like:

Welcome to Ubuntu ...
ubuntu@ip-172-31-44-24:~$

Don't close this PowerShell window.

The tunnel is now active.























































